- Given the following YAML
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
      volumes:
      - name: data
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: my-pvc  # Name of the Persistent Volume Claim

- We would expect the following tables to be built

(.)
| apiVersion | kind       | metadata | spec |
| apps/v1    | Deployment | ->       | ->   |

(.metadata)
| name             |
| nginx-deployment |

(.spec)
| replicas | selector | template |
| 3        | ->       | ->       |

(.spec.selector)
| matchLabels |
| ->          |

(.spec.selector.matchLabels)
| app   |
| nginx |

(.spec.template)
| metadata | containers | volumes |
| ->       | ->         | ->      |

(.spec.template.metadata)
| labels |
| ->     |

(.spec.template.metadata.labels)
| app   |
| nginx |

(.spec.template.containers)
| name  | image        | ports |
| nginx | nginx:latest | ->    |

(.spec.template.containers.ports)
| containerPort |
| 80 |

(.spec.template.volumes)
| name | persistentVolumeClaim |
| data | ->                    |

(.spec.template.volumes.persistentVolumeClaim)
| claimName |
| my-pvc    |

- Tried import the yaml with a ruby script and the loaded object looks like:
```
{
    "apiVersion"=>"apps/v1",
    "kind"=>"Deployment",
    "metadata"=>{
        "name"=>"nginx-deployment"
    },
    "spec"=>{
        "replicas"=>3,
        "selector"=>{
            "matchLabels"=>{
                "app"=>"nginx"
            }
        },
        "template"=>{
            "metadata"=>{
                "labels"=>{
                    "app"=>"nginx"
                }
            },
            "spec"=>{
                "containers"=>[{
                    "name"=>"nginx",
                    "image"=>"nginx:latest",
                    "ports"=>[{
                        "containerPort"=>80
                    }]
                }],
                "volumes"=>[{
                    "name"=>"data",
                    "persistentVolumeClaim"=>{
                        "claimName"=>"my-pvc"
                    }
                }]
            }
        }
    }
}

- So, each object is a ruby hash ("key" => "value").
- MAYBE, we could say that if the value of the hash is an object, we create a new table from that object?


